ClearOneShot(): return early when no debugger is attached

Debug::ClearOneShot() walks every DebugInfo of the isolate on each thread
re-entry (ThreadManager::RestoreThread -> Debug::RestoreDebug -> ClearOneShot)
and clears and re-applies the break points of each of them. Block coverage keeps
a DebugInfo for every function that has run - that is where the coverage
counters live - so on a long run whose isolate is re-entered from many threads
the walk costs O(functions) per entry whether or not a single break point
exists.

One-shot break points are what stepping sets, and stepping needs a debugger, so
with no debugger attached the walk has nothing to do: the work of each DebugInfo
is already guarded (ClearBreakPoints returns unless there is instrumented
bytecode with break info) and the only unconditional side effect left,
ApplyBreakPoints' SetDebugExecutionMode(kBreakpoints), is debugger state that
nothing reads without a debugger. is_active_ is set exactly by
Debug::SetDebugDelegate() and V8 itself uses !is_active_ as its "no debugger"
predicate (Debug::ignore_events()).

Measured on agent-app with block coverage on (packages/all.test.js, 6,322
cases): 43 minutes and still no progress, 4 budget failures, before - 7m46s,
6,322/6,322 passed, 0 failures, after. The cases that used to stall pass in
2.2s / 0.2s / 0.5s (reclaims an idle project directory (repo.git) /
removeProject clears metadata and index rows / keep host view reads and bulk
uploads within a reasonable local budget). Coverage output is byte identical
with and without this patch on five workloads.

Apply from the v8 directory:

    cd vender && git apply v8/patch/clearonesshot-fastpath.patch

See plans/cov-overhead-report-2026-09-29.md (R9/R10) in the fibjs tree.

diff --git a/v8/src/debug/debug.cc b/v8/src/debug/debug.cc
index ff4314488..f61b4cb39 100644
--- a/v8/src/debug/debug.cc
+++ b/v8/src/debug/debug.cc
@@ -1666,6 +1666,13 @@ void Debug::ClearStepping() {
 // are used to support stepping.
 void Debug::ClearOneShot() {
   RCS_SCOPE(isolate_, RuntimeCallCounterId::kDebugger);
+  // One-shot break points are what stepping sets, and stepping needs a
+  // debugger: without one there is nothing here to clear. The walk below goes
+  // through every DebugInfo of the isolate, and block coverage keeps a
+  // DebugInfo for every function that has run - so on a long run whose isolate
+  // is re-entered from many threads this walk is the most expensive part of the
+  // run, at O(functions) per entry whether or not a single break point exists.
+  if (!is_active()) return;
   // The current implementation just runs through all the breakpoints. When the
   // last break point for a function is removed that function is automatically
   // removed from the list.
